<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Venta</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 900–400 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Venta is an archaeological site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmecs">Olmec civilisation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabasco">Tabasco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, on an island in a coastal swamp, which was the pre-eminent Olmec centre from after 900 BCE until it was all but abandoned around 400 BCE. Built largely of earth and clay, it has a Great Pyramid 34 metres high, one of the earliest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olmec-colossal-heads">colossal heads</a> and buried offerings of serpentine blocks. Described by Frans Blom and Oliver La Farge in 1925, it was excavated from 1940 by Matthew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stirling">Stirling</a> and Philip Drucker.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-venta.jpg" alt="La Venta" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfonsobouchot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Venta_Pir%C3%A1mide_cara_norte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
